---
source: tests/snapshots_help_contracts.rs
expression: "help(&scratch, None, &[\"--help\", \"worktree\", \"prune\"])"
---
Usage: worktree prune [--repo PATH] [--stale-days N] [--release-stale --reason TEXT] [--dryrun] [--json|--format json] [--verbose] [--remove] [--no-sync]

Global, removal-by-default cleanup of managed worktrees, grouped by canonical directory. Without --repo it enumerates every repository identity the local lease store records (even outside Git) and resolves each through its main checkout, skipping an identity it cannot verify with a bounded reason instead of guessing; --repo PATH restricts the pass to one verified checkout. A directory is removed as soon as every issue associated with it is confirmed closed through its own durable task (a legacy number requires proven sidecar or link ownership, or an explicit trusted manual selection), no active lease references the canonical directory, and prune's running-session guard clears it: only a session the connected shared foreground-drain service reports as *currently running* in the directory blocks removal, checked read-only, so an inactive historical session record that still points at the directory never keeps it. The read-only classification never enumerates historical sessions and never moves a session; the preview then reads each candidate's fresh occupancy and the lineage the host reports, so it reports the same blockers and the same move eligibility the removal would meet, without moving, deleting, or writing anything; coverage is that one connected service, not a whole-machine multi-service inventory. The removal stage is the only place that may move a session: before it deletes a directory it reads that directory's fresh occupancy and the lineage the host reports, and returns every associated idle `ses_` session — the sessions its leases name and the descendants that descend from them — to the repository's verified main checkout, confirming each move with a fresh authoritative read. Every safe family member is requested once before any confirmation wait: an immediately pending request no longer suppresses a later sibling. The first read after each request is always immediate; only an actual prune may then poll all still-pending accepted moves round-robin inside one invocation-shared 3-second scheduling budget (100 ms apart, never reset per directory or session, never reissuing a move), and the preview and close never wait. A pending move that does not arrive within the budget is reported as waiting and keeps the directory. One narrow dirty exception is shared with the close and reconciliation cleanup: a candidate whose only untracked content is an exact root `.cache` real directory is removable when Git tracks nothing below it, porcelain status names only `??` paths under that root, and a bounded symlink-free inventory (at most 4096 entries) of reliable device/inode and high-resolution change evidence re-reads identically immediately before deletion, with the initial eligibility baseline carried across host/session movement; a platform without reliable identity fails closed for this exception; any tracked, staged, modified, renamed, unmerged, other-untracked, `.cache2`, nested `other/.cache`, file, symlink, cross-filesystem, unreadable, over-bound, same-length replacement, root replacement, or otherwise changed inventory keeps the directory, and the ordinary no-force `git worktree remove` still follows only after every applicable lease, local, closure, and fresh host gate is re-checked after cache clearance. Those checks describe the one connected service at that moment, not a guarantee about session changes after them. A session no lease of this repository associates, and no lineage reaches one of them, keeps the directory and is never moved; so do an unavailable or incomplete occupancy listing, an unresolvable associated session, an answer that names another session or reports an unusable location, an unreadable, mismatched, or cyclic lineage, a rejected, queued, or unconfirmed move, an unverifiable main checkout, and a session that started running in the meantime. An unavailable, malformed, mismatched, or non-absolute running-session answer, or any running session (root or child) at the directory, keeps it; removal then uses `git worktree remove` without `--force` after a fresh re-check of the running-session answer and fresh lease rows. There is no waiting period: a freshly retained closed clean directory is removed immediately, so --stale-days never gates directory deletion and exists only for the explicit --release-stale recovery below. A recorded worktree path that no longer exists on disk is counted as absent and its still-open lease rows converge once to `released`; the path is historical lease evidence, not an OpenCode registration state, and an already-released row is never rewritten by a later run. Closure is read per candidate issue through its own durable task, which pins its provider and instance, or through proven sidecar or unique link ownership. Legacy Redmine scopes use the trusted global instance; Local reads its existing store; Forgejo legacy scopes also require the physical origin to attest the trusted instance and repository. A scopeless number keeps unless an explicit trusted manual selection proves its route. The default provider never supplies ownership. A missing, unavailable, ambiguous, or mismatched issue is never treated as closed, and a preview may read closure but never creates or migrates a store. The local guards — never the main checkout, never a path an `active` lease points at, never dirty or untracked, except the one explicit root `.cache` exception below — are decided before any running-session query, so a main-checkout or dirty directory makes zero host or provider calls. Because the runtime-host dependency is explicit, an unreadable service keeps even a pure-CLI candidate; the human failure count scopes to failed `git worktree remove` calls rather than to kept directories, which can include an unproven running-session answer. --dryrun (alias --dry-run) is the read-only preview: it performs the same read-only running-session discovery to report eligibility and then reads each candidate's occupancy and lineage, so it reports the blockers and the move eligibility the removal would meet, but never mutates any directory, lease row, or session and never moves a session. --release-stale requires a non-empty --reason unless it is a preview and flips exactly the active leases whose heartbeat is older than --stale-days to `retained` in a single transaction; a directory that held an active lease at invocation start is never removed by the same call, and --reason without --release-stale is rejected. The default output follows the global --output auto selection: the compact human summary on an interactive terminal and the machine-readable aggregate envelope on a pipe or in CI, while --json or --format json still forces the envelope, --format text forces the summary, and a legacy selection that contradicts an explicit --output is rejected. The summary is one heading, one aggregate count line, one kept-reason counts line that splits active, main, waiting-move, other-protected, issue-state, and unknown with arithmetic matching the kept total, at most five removal-failure examples with their path and reason plus the omitted count, a bounded absent-description line when historical lease paths no longer exist on disk, a skipped-repository count when nonzero, and the measured elapsed time; its size never grows with the number of directories, because it lists no per-directory row. --verbose keeps that summary and adds one bounded row per actual removal, per removal failure, per eligible preview row (never labelled as removed), per absent directory, and per kept directory with its reason; it changes nothing in the machine document. The aggregate envelope (per repository plus totals) is the complete-detail inspection channel, since the summary never lists every absent or no-op path. --remove is an accepted compatibility no-op and --no-sync is accepted but no longer runs a sync pass: prune never syncs. Branches and lease rows are never deleted. Progress is a human affordance: on an interactive terminal one stderr line is redrawn in place through scanning, verification and deletion, showing completed repositories against the known repository total and cumulative completed directory counts for removal, absence, retention and failure, never a directory path or completion before an action returns, and it is cleared before the result, while a machine run (JSON, a pipe, CI, a dumb terminal, an agent role, or --no-progress) never sees a progress line; actual warnings and errors keep their stderr channel, and stdout stays reserved for the final summary or the single JSON document; within one invocation the read-only scan shares a repeated running-session answer and resolves each repository's identity once, but every removal re-checks the running-session answer, closure, and lease rows live and reads the fresh occupancy of the directory it is about to remove. Orchestrator-only.
